  "response": "**Most likely cause**: `src/date.ts` — three findings stack into a textbook date-boundary bug, and billing.ts almost certainly routes through it:\n\n- **crime_00003 (Half-UTC, Half-Local)** — the receiver `d` uses `getUTCFullYear()` at L9 but `getMonth()` at L41. The two families disagree by the host's UTC offset, so any value that straddles midnight UTC reads as a different month/year depending on where the test runs.\n- **crime_00009 (Timezone Roulette)** — `new Date(\"2026-12-25T07:00:00\")` and `new Date(\"2026-12-20\")` parse with no zone. Per ECMA-262, date-only is UTC and date+time is local, so the two literals on lines 29 and 33 land in *different* time domains. CI in UTC and your laptop in -08:00 will land on different days.\n- **crime_00011 (DST-Naive Day Math)** — `+ 86400000` at L52 assumes a day is 24h. On DST-transition days the result lands at 23:00 or 01:00 of the next day — boundary tests flake twice a year.\n\n`generateInvoice` (crime_00019, 7× `Date.now()`/`new Date()` direct) compounds this: tests can't pin a moment to even isolate which of the three is firing.\n\n**Fix, in order**:\n1. In `src/date.ts`, convert `d` to all-UTC reads (`getUTCMonth()` at L41) and append `Z` to both literals (`\"2026-12-25T07:00:00Z\"`, `\"2026-12-20T00:00:00Z\"`).\n2. Replace `+ 86400000` with a calendar-aware addDays (Luxon / Temporal / date-fns-tz).\n3. Thread a `clock: () => Date` parameter through `generateInvoice` so the test can pin time and reproduce the boundary deterministically.\n\n(1) is the likely direct cause of *this* failure; (2) prevents the DST-day version of the same bug; (3) is what stops the next flake from being mysterious.",
